winch secret decrypt: print the plaintext of a secret

Examples:
  # print the plaintext of the default of a variable
  winch secret decrypt vars.monit_password
  # print the plaintext of a secret in the vars of one host
  winch secret decrypt hosts.db1.acme.com.vars.db_password
  # print the plaintext of a !secret value
  echo '!secret YWdl...' | winch secret decrypt

Usage:
  winch secret decrypt [<path>]

Decrypt prints the plaintext of the !secret at the path, or of a
!secret value on stdin, with or without the tag. It prints nothing
else, also no newline after the plaintext. It is the only command whose
output holds a secret.

A path names a place in the project, with keys joined by ".".
vars.x is the default of x in winch.yaml, and recipes.mail.vars.mail_x
a default in the recipe mail. groups.all.vars.x and hosts.web1.vars.x
are in the inventory at the top of winch.yaml, and
inventories.prod.groups.all.vars.x is in the inventory prod. A path can
go on into a map variable, such as vars.users.alice, but not into a
list. A key with dots or "[" stands in brackets and double quotes,
such as vars.dkim_keys["acme.com"].

A path to a map or to a value that is not a !secret is an error.

winch reads the age key from the stdout of the command in
WINCH_AGE_IDENTITY_CMD, which runs through sh -c. Without it, winch
reads the file that WINCH_AGE_KEY names, or else
~/.config/winch/age.key. Both variables set is an error.

See: winch doc secrets
